{"name":"Romantic Ballad","hex":"#e0bedf","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"romantic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a work of literature, a writer etc.: being like or having the characteristics of a romance, or poetic tale of a mythic or quasi-historical time; fantastic. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Fictitious, imaginary."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A person with romantic character (a character like those of the knights in a mythic romance).","A person who is behaving romantically (in a manner befitting someone who feels an idealized form of love)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/romantic"},{"term":"ballad","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A kind of narrative poem, adapted for recitation or singing; especially, a sentimental or romantic poem in short stanzas.","A slow romantic song."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make mention of in ballads.","To compose or sing ballads."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ballad"}],"entries":[{"title":"Latin ballad","blocks":[{"text":"Latin ballad is a sentimental ballad derived from bolero that originated in the early 1960s in Los Angeles, California and Southern California."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Latin_ballad"},{"title":"Ballad","blocks":[{"text":"A ballad is a form of verse, often a narrative set to music. Ballads were particularly characteristic of the popular poetry and song of England, Ireland and Scotland from the Late Middle Ages until the 19th century. They were widely used across Europe, and later in Australia, North Africa, North America and South America."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Maria_Wiik_-_Ballad.jpg/330px-Maria_Wiik_-_Ballad.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ballad"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Here_begynneth